            {/* Section: The Real Difference */}
            <section id="the-real-difference" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
              <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                  What Is The Real Difference Between Ben Dinh And Ben Duoc?
                </h2>
              </div>

              <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
                <p>
                  The foundational divergence between the two sites stems from their distinct functions during the war:
                </p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-600 text-sm">
                  <li><strong>Ben Dinh</strong> served as the command base of the <strong>Cu Chi District Party Committee</strong>. It was a frontline partisan stronghold located within the crosshairs of Operation Crimp (1966) and Operation Cedar Falls (1967).</li>
                  <li><strong>Ben Duoc</strong> housed the <strong>Military Command of the Saigon–Gia Dinh Special Zone</strong> (<em>Khu ủy Sài Gòn – Gia Định</em>). Because it sheltered the highest strategic leadership echelon of the southern resistance, it required a vastly more intricate, deeply subterranean system of command bunkers, operating rooms, and escape arteries connecting to the Saigon River.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                  When the sites were developed for historical tourism following national reunification, their geographical profiles shaped their modern format:
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Ben Dinh</strong>, located on Provincial Road 15 and closer to the urban perimeter, was organized into a concise, easily walkable circuit capable of handling high daily turnover.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Ben Duoc</strong>, embedded within a rural forest preserve farther out, was developed into a comprehensive historical, botanical, and commemorative park encompassing the Ben Duoc Memorial Temple and the Reconstructed Liberation Zone.
                </p>
              </div>

