                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            {/* Section: Authenticity Myth */}
            <section id="authenticity-myth" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
              <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                  Is Ben Duoc Really "More Authentic" Than Ben Dinh?
                </h2>
              </div>

              <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
                <p>
                  One of the most persistent claims on travel forums is that Ben Dinh is an artificial "replica" built strictly for tourists, while Ben Duoc represents the "real" historical tunnels.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>This assertion is factually incorrect.</strong>
                </p>
                <p>
                  Both Ben Dinh and Ben Duoc are authentic historical sectors of the National Special Relic of Cu Chi Tunnels. In Vietnam’s official nomination dossier submitted to the <strong>UNESCO World Heritage Centre on July 15, 2026</strong>, both Ben Dinh and Ben Duoc are specifically cited as the core surviving sections that retain the genuine structure, scale, and three-tiered architectural depth of the original wartime network.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Both systems feature a sophisticated three-tiered structural design, but UNESCO’s 2026 archival survey notes that Ben Dinh is shallower than Ben Duoc:
                </p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-600 text-sm">
                  <li><strong>Tier 1:</strong> 3 to 4 meters below the surface (sheltering rifle pits and ventilation shafts).</li>
                  <li><strong>Tier 2:</strong> 5 to 6 meters underground (housing surgical wards, meeting rooms, and kitchens).</li>
                  <li><strong>Tier 3:</strong> 8 to 10 meters deep (providing bomb-proof sanctuary capable of withstanding heavy artillery and B-52 strikes).</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                  Furthermore, <strong>both sites have been adapted for public safety</strong>. Original tunnels measured roughly 60–70 cm high by 40–50 cm wide—dimensions through which an average modern Western traveler could not physically pass. Select visitor sections at both Ben Dinh and Ben Duoc were reinforced with brick, subtly widened, and equipped with low-wattage electrical lighting and emergency exit hatches spaced every 10 to 20 meters.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            {/* Section: Ben Dinh Convenience */}
