              <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
                <p>
                  If Ben Dinh is an efficient classroom, <strong>Ben Duoc is an expansive living monument</strong>.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Located in Phu My Hung Commune along the banks of the Saigon River, Ben Duoc encompasses a vast historical complex. Its focal point is the solemn <strong>Ben Duoc Martyrs Memorial Temple</strong> (<em>Đền Tưởng niệm Liệt sĩ Bến Dược</em>), inaugurated in 1995. Built in traditional three-bay Vietnamese pagoda architecture with curved tiled roofs, the sanctuary honors over <strong>44,522 fallen soldiers, partisans, and civilians</strong> whose names are engraved in gold across stone tablets lining the temple walls.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Key distinctions at Ben Duoc include:
                </p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-600 text-sm">
                  <li><strong>Significantly less commercial crowding:</strong> Large group buses rarely make the 70 km drive, preserving a tranquil forest canopy.</li>
                  <li><strong>Deeper, multi-tiered tunnels:</strong> Preserved tunnels descend into the second and third levels (down to 10 meters).</li>
                  <li><strong>Recreated Liberation Zone:</strong> A life-sized outdoor museum recreating southern wartime rural villages, complete with thatched stilt houses, rice-milling workshops, and artisan looms.</li>
                  <li><strong>Spacious riverside park:</strong> Open green spaces, shaded walking boulevards, and electric shuttle carts for visitors seeking mobility assistance.</li>
                </ul>
